Ship 12: rows our own label struck return on what their own source says (relay v207, in part); 62 lines move, all in the Tikkunei Zohar

The corpus lane's relay v207 (20 November 2026) judges again, row by row, the sources the language admission struck on the label this project gave them. This ship lands two of its three lists: PanLex's and English Wiktionary's translation tables' rows whose own language field names Hebrew or Aramaic (4,090 rows), and Harkavy's rows, each a word of his Hebrew column with his English column (376). The store goes from 653,227 rows to 657,693. The third list, the Bible dictionaries (Davidson's seventeen other sources, the ETCBC's glosses, STEP's Expanded Strong tags; 18,164 rows), is proved and held for the owner's word. The record: wip/2026-11-21/v207/REHEARSAL.md and PLAN.md.

WHY ONLY PART OF THE RELAY

- A trial landed all three lists on a copy and read every resting line against live. In the thirty-nine books nothing moved. In the Tikkunei Zohar, which the rest does not reach, 1,276 lines moved, 1,111 of them by the ETCBC's rows: they come back at the low ranks they held, and among readings of one year the lower rank leads («not» for «power» at 288 places, «Zaccur» for «remember»; also «Elijah» for «Elihu»).
- About a fifth of Davidson's returning rows are descriptions of a word and not readings of it («a masculine proper name», 445 rows). Resting open with the owner's twelve they would add pills at about 101,700 word places of the books.
- The owner's rule is «slowly add». So each of the three waits for the owner to add it; data/readmission-v1.json names the nineteen sources as held, with why.

THE RE-ADMISSION (readmission-rule-v1, new)

- The admission's own record says, of a source struck for silence, that «the fix is a label at the source naming what its own title page names, not an exception here», and the strike tool says the way back is for the source to carry the languages its own source names. Relay v207 is that fix. data/readmission-v1.json names the sixteen sources that return, each with what its own source says, the languages that names, and its index entry as the store held it before the strike (recovered from the history). tools/readmit-sources-v1.mjs puts the entry back with those languages and no wording year (the lane: no ruling gives any of them one), moves the source from the struck to the re-admitted in the index, and marks it in the admission's record. Run again it writes nothing.
- The strike's own figures stand: 49 sources were struck; 33 are struck now and 16 stand re-admitted.
- A source returns whole (Harkavy's twelve) or for its listed rows alone, where each row's own field decides (PanLex's two, the translation tables' two: 3,956 and 134 rows back, 10,052 staying out by their own field). Only the landings write rows. tools/project-lattice-v12-v1.mjs takes a lattice card of a source back for its listed rows alone as the headword's line only where a served row of that source speaks it.
- tools/strike-language-v1.mjs keeps a re-admission: run again it had rebuilt its record without it and written all 49 ids back into the index's struck list. Run dry now: 162 sources to 162, no row struck.

THE LANDINGS AT A STRIKE'S HOLES (landings-rule-v1, the same rule)

- These lists are lists of holes (a_named_rank_is in the pin): a line's rank is the hole its row left when its source was struck. tools/derive-landings-v1.mjs holds each to the struck-ranks record (that rank, of that source, under that key) and lands the row at it where no row stands there now: 4,302 rows. Where another row stands at the hole (9), or the list names no rank (155), the row stands after the last row and the last typed hole of its key.
- That last clause departs from the lane's words («the highest served rank under the key plus one»). Read so, six lines of the first list took holes that later lines of the same list name. A hole by strike is kept for the row that left it (struck-rank-rule-v1), so a row with no hole of its own stands past the typed holes too. Put to the corpus lane.
- A large list is kept gzipped and weighed as the bytes it holds, the figures the lane's letter gives (tools/pinned-v1.mjs); tools/strike-rows-v1.mjs reads such a list.
- No line is left unlanded and none is gathered. 114 keys are new to the store.

WHAT THE RETURNING SOURCES ARE FILED AS

- The kinds record (version 2026-11-21a): PanLex's two keys and the translation tables' key join their families, word lists, which rest shut on the thirty-nine books by their kind. Harkavy is a new family, not filed under a kind, shut with every dictionary that is not a Bible dictionary. So nothing of this ship shows at rest in the books; opened, the rows stand as any shut dictionary's do.
- The ledgers' manifest says why each returning source has no ledger (excluded): the lane has declared none, so each row is one reading as its landing line gives it.

EVERY RESTING LINE AGAINST LIVE: 62 ENTRIES MOVE IN 40 WORKS, ALL IN THE TIKKUNEI ZOHAR, IN SIX SPELLINGS

- 58 bare words gain a line: «throne» at 33 places and «bed» at 1 (the translation tables' Aramaic), «in the manner of» at 3 and «jolting» at 1 (Harkavy), and «wolf» at 20 (PanLex's Aramaic).
- «wolf» is known and shipped as known: the letters do spell the Aramaic word for a wolf, and in the Zohar they are «of the father», a prefix and a word. The Zohar's prefix split is an open ruling; until it is made a whole spelling reads as one word there, as at every other such place.
- 4 keep their word, «month», and change their credit.

THE CHECKS

- check-readmission-v1 (new), four laws: R1 the record against the landings' pin and its lists; R2 the index (each entry as the record returns it and admitted by its languages; the struck and the re-admitted together the 49, each once); R3 the admission's record; R4 every row of a returning source is a landed line's, so a source back for its listed rows alone carries no other, and a struck or held source carries none. On the trial tree R3 named a stale record.
- check-landings-v1 G2 holds a list of holes to its rule; before it learned it, it named exactly the 9 lines whose hole another row holds.
- check-language-admitted-v1 judges a re-admitted source by the languages its own source names, as the strike tool reads them, and every other source by its label as before; the cumulative figures count the struck with the re-admitted.
- The lane state names the re-admitted beside the struck, and check-lane-state-v1 S3 holds the three to one another.

